
<% content_for :title, "Mania & Hypomania Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-mania">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / MANIA &amp; HYPOMANIA
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>FEELING</span>
        <span>FUCKING</span>
        <span>INVINCIBLE?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR PLAN FOR STAYING SOBER
        WHEN YOUR MOOD HEADS UP.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR PATTERNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR PATTERNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHEN UP<br>
        STARTS SHIFTING.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Mania doesn't always begin with obvious
          chaos. Sometimes it starts with feeling
          unusually good. You're energized,
          motivated, confident, and full of ideas.
          Everything seems interesting, and
          you suddenly have plans for all of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your thoughts move faster, your need
          for sleep decreases, and the distance
          between having an idea and acting
          on it gets smaller. What would
          normally require consideration
          suddenly feels obvious.
        </p>

        <p>
          Spending can become particularly
          tempting. You see something you
          want, and buying it feels like
          the natural next step. You
          start projects, make commitments,
          and convince yourself that
          everything will work out
          because you're certain it will.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol can fit into that same
          pattern. Maybe drinking sounds
          fun, exciting, or like the perfect
          addition to an already good mood.
          Or maybe your thoughts are moving
          so quickly that you want
          something to slow them down.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might start making exceptions
          to your sobriety: just one drink,
          just this once, or just because
          you're feeling good. The
          familiar "fuck it" can arrive
          before you've considered
          what happens next. Or the opposite. 
          Your thoughts are racing so fast you will do 
          anything just to shut them up.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hypomania can involve similar
          changes without the same severity
          as full mania. Neither should
          be treated as harmless simply
          because you're enjoying the feeling.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Feeling confident doesn't mean
            your judgment is reliable.
            The time to recognize mania
            is before it starts
            making decisions for you.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / YOUR WARNING SIGNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / YOUR WARNING SIGNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT CHANGES<br>
        FOR YOU?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- SLEEP & ENERGY -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>SLEEP &amp; ENERGY</h3>

          <p>
            Watch for sleeping significantly
            less without feeling tired,
            staying awake late because
            your mind won't slow down,
            or waking up with
            more energy than usual.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may feel driven to
            keep moving, talking,
            researching, creating,
            cleaning, or working
            on multiple projects.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention when you
            feel too energized
            to rest, or when
            sleeping seems like
            an unnecessary interruption.
          </p>

          <p>
            A reduced need for sleep
            is an important warning
            sign.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- THOUGHTS & CONFIDENCE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>THOUGHTS &amp; CONFIDENCE</h3>

          <p>
            Your thoughts race,
            ideas connect rapidly,
            and everything seems
            unusually clear or
            significant. They will
            not shut up. They keep going
            and going and going. 
          </p>

          <p>
            You may feel more
            capable, persuasive,
            creative, or certain
            than usual. Doubt
            starts feeling unnecessary,
            and anyone questioning
            your plans may seem
            irritating or unreasonable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for the conviction
            that you have to act
            immediately. Not every
            idea needs to become
            a project, purchase,
            or commitment.
          </p>

          

          <p>
            <strong>
              Certainty is a feeling.
              It isn't proof.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- BEHAVIOR & DECISIONS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>BEHAVIOR &amp; DECISIONS</h3>

          <p>
            Spending increases.
            You browse things
            to buy, justify
            purchases, or decide
            that expensive ideas
            are suddenly affordable.
          </p>

          <p>
            You start new projects
            before finishing existing
            ones. You make plans,
            contact people impulsively,
            or take on commitments
            without considering
            the time, money,
            or energy involved.
          </p>

          <p>
            You might talk faster,
            interrupt more,
            become impatient,
            or get unusually angry
            when something slows
            you down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when you're
            acting first and
            thinking afterward.
            The smaller the
            gap between impulse
            and action, the
            more important
            external safeguards
            become. Sound familiar? (Third DUI)
          </p>

          <p>
            Risky driving,
            drinking, and
            other high-consequence
            decisions deserve
            particular attention.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- DRINKING ARGUMENT -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>THE DRINKING ARGUMENT</h3>

          <p>
            "I'm feeling great.
            What's the harm?"
          </p>

          <p>
            "I've been sober
            long enough."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I can handle it
            this time."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I deserve to
            celebrate."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I just need
            something to help
            me slow down."
          </p>

          <p>
            "Fuck it."
          </p>

          <p>
            These arguments may
            feel completely logical
            when your mood is elevated.
            That's exactly why
            you shouldn't judge
            them only by how
            convincing they feel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your sobriety isn't
            something to renegotiate
            every time your mood
            changes. You already
            know what alcohol
            has cost you.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Feeling invincible
              doesn't make
              you immune to
              consequences.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR RESPONSE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR RESPONSE
      </p>

      <h2>
        MAKE THE PLAN<br>
        BEFORE THE PITCH.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- FIRST STEPS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR FIRST STEPS</h3>

          <p>
            When you notice the
            warning signs, acknowledge
            them instead of immediately
            explaining them away.
            You don't have to
            feel out of control
            for a mood episode
            to be developing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your sleep,
            energy, spending,
            irritability, and
            impulses against
            your usual baseline.
            Look at patterns,
            not just how
            you feel today.
          </p>

          <p>
            Continue taking
            your medication
            exactly as prescribed.
            Don't skip doses,
            change medications,
            or adjust dosages
            without guidance
            from your psych.
          </p>

         

          <p>
            Prioritize a consistent
            sleep schedule and
            reduce unnecessary
            stimulation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Delay major decisions,
            purchases, commitments,
            and emotionally charged
            messages until your
            mood is stable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              You don't have
              to wait for mania
              to become severe
              before responding.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- ENVIRONMENT -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR ENVIRONMENT</h3>

          <p>
            Make impulsive decisions
            harder to act on.
            Remove easy access
            to alcohol and avoid
            places where drinking
            is the main activity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't browse alcohol
            delivery or make
            exceptions to sobriety
            because you're excited
            or feeling unusually
            confident.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put practical limits
            on spending. Close
            shopping apps, pause
            major purchases, and
            consider asking a
            trusted person to
            help you protect
            essential money
            while your mood
            is elevated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep your schedule
            manageable. You don't
            need to accept every
            invitation, start
            every project, or
            follow every exciting
            idea.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write ideas down
            without acting on
            them immediately.
            Let them exist
            without turning
            each one into
            a commitment.
          </p>


          <p>
            <strong>
              Build barriers
              while you can
              still recognize
              why you need them.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / YOUR SUPPORT PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / YOUR SUPPORT PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHO GETS<br>
        THE HEADS-UP?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- TRUSTED PEOPLE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>PEOPLE YOU TRUST</h3>

          <p>
            Choose people who
            recognize changes
            in your behavior
            and are willing
            to tell you
            when something
            seems different.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let them know
            your warning signs:
            reduced sleep,
            racing thoughts,
            impulsive spending,
            unusual confidence,
            irritability,
            and risky decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can say:
            "I'm noticing some
            signs that my mood
            might be getting
            elevated. I'm
            feeling unusually
            energized and
            impulsive. Can
            you help me
            keep an eye
            on it?"
          </p>

         

          <p>
            If you're tempted
            to drink, say
            that directly.
            Don't wait until
            you've already
            bought alcohol
            to involve
            your recovery
            support.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your first person
            is unavailable,
            contact another
            trusted person.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Someone noticing
              a warning sign
              isn't automatically
              criticizing you.
              Sometimes they're
              seeing something
              you're having
              trouble recognizing.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


       <!-- BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to relapse plans">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS",
      toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav> 

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
